-- GF-8: the index the tenant-scoped alert list is ordered by.
--
-- Generated by `prisma migrate diff --from-config-datasource --to-schema`
-- against the migrated disposable database, then edited by hand for exactly one
-- reason, recorded here in full:
--
--   Prisma emitted
--     ALTER TABLE "Geofence" ALTER COLUMN "centerPoint" DROP NOT NULL,
--       ALTER COLUMN "centerPoint" DROP DEFAULT;
--     ALTER TABLE "LocationEvent" ALTER COLUMN "observedPoint" DROP NOT NULL,
--       ALTER COLUMN "observedPoint" DROP DEFAULT;
--   and both blocks were REMOVED.
--
--   Neither is a GF-8 change. They are the known, documented GF-3/GF-4 drift:
--   Prisma models the geography columns as optional `Unsupported(...)` fields and
--   cannot represent a STORED GENERATED column, so on every schema change it
--   re-proposes stripping the NOT NULL and the generation expression that make
--   scalar/spatial divergence impossible. PostgreSQL refuses the statement
--   outright ("column is a generated column"). The same removal was made, for the
--   same reason, in the GF-4 `tracked_devices_and_location_events`, GF-6
--   `geofence_device_transition_state` and GF-7 `geofence_alert_events`
--   migrations; the drift is still asserted verbatim by
--   scripts/disposable-db-test.mjs and is not suppressed here, only kept out of a
--   migration that has nothing to do with it.
--
-- The CreateIndex below is Prisma's own output, unmodified.
--
-- Why an index at all. GF-8 is otherwise a pure read phase over the GF-7 table
-- and adds no column, constraint, table or trigger. But its primary access path —
-- one tenant's alerts, newest observation first — is served by nothing that
-- already exists:
--
--   * "AlertEvent_crossing_key" leads with (tenantId, trackedDeviceId) and does
--     not contain "observedAt" at all;
--   * "AlertEvent_tenantId_sourceLocationEventId_idx" and
--     "AlertEvent_tenantId_geofenceId_idx" each serve one exact filter and can
--     order by neither the observation instant nor the tie-breaker.
--
-- Without this index the unfiltered list is a scan of every alert the tenant has
-- ever recorded followed by a sort, so its cost grows with the tenant's whole
-- history rather than with the page returned — the one shape of query GF-8 makes
-- routine. The three existing indexes are untouched and still serve the filtered
-- lookups they were created for.
--
-- Both components descend, matching ORDER BY "observedAt" DESC, "id" DESC
-- exactly, so the ordering is satisfied by a forward scan. "id" is in the index
-- because it is the unique tie-breaker that makes the order total: "observedAt"
-- alone is not unique — one observation crosses several geofences at one instant
-- — and an unstable order within a tie is what puts one alert on two adjacent
-- pages.
--
-- Deployment note: this is a plain CREATE INDEX, which takes a SHARE lock and
-- blocks writes to "AlertEvent" (that is, ingestion of crossings) while it
-- builds. On a large existing table, build it as CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY
-- outside Migrate instead — Migrate runs each migration in a transaction, and
-- CONCURRENTLY cannot run inside one.

-- CreateIndex
CREATE INDEX "AlertEvent_tenantId_observedAt_id_idx" ON "AlertEvent"("tenantId", "observedAt" DESC, "id" DESC);
